INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/crustacea/crustacea.md', 'crustacea', 'Crustaceans', 'published', '
  
Crustaceans are among our favourite seafood! Having to personally dismantle some of
these creatures to eat them, many of us are more familiar with them
than we might imagine.  
  
## What are crustaceans?
If crustaceans remind you of insects, they are indeed related and belong to the same
phylum. Together with more familiar creatures like spiders and
centipedes, they all belong to the Phylum [Arthropoda](/entries/Arthropoda/).
Crustaceans are the largest group of marine arthropods. There are
about 45,000 known species of crustaceans. More than 80% of these
are marine.  
  
Arthropods have a hardened outer skeleton (exoskeleton) called the
cuticle. They have jointed legs. Arthropods make up nine-tenths of
all known animals, so this body plan seems to work well.

{% include img-grid.html items=page.galleries.gallery1%}

Crustaceans come in a wide range of body forms. Some adults are so
unlike ''typical'' crustaceans that it is hard to believe they are related
to familiar creatures like shrimps and crabs. These include [barnacles](othercrust/ciriipedia/cirripedia),
often mistaken for oysters or limpets, and [parasitic
barnacles](othercrust/ciriipedia/thompsonia) that invisible for most of their lifecycle! [Sea
slaters](othercrust/isopoda/ligia) scurry about by the hundreds over rocks at low tide and
are often mistaken for insects or cockroaches. They are, however,
crustaceans just like crabs and prawns.  
  
One crustacean characteristic that distinguish them from other arthropods
is that at some time in their life cycle, crustaceans have two pairs
of antennae. All other arthropod groups only have one pair. Most crustaceans
have limbs that are branched (biramous), while other arthropods don''t.  
  
**Classifying Crustacea:** Nearly
60% of crustaceans fall into the Class Malacostraca. Most malacostracans
(10,000 species) belong to the Order Decapoda.   
  
**Class Malacostraca**  
- [**Order Decapoda**](decapoda)
  - Brachyurans [true crabs](decapoda)  
  - Anomurans [hermit crabs](othercrust/anomura/hermit/hermit); [porcelain crabs](othercrust/anomura/porcellanidae/porcellanidae)  
  - [prawns and shrimps](othercrust/shrimp/shrimp), [lobsters](othercrust/lobster/lobster) 
- **Order Stomatopoda** [mantis shrimps](othercrust/stomatopoda/stomatopoda)  
- **Order Amphipoda** [amphipods](othercrust/amphipoda/amphipoda)  
- **Order Isopoda** [sea slaters](othercrust/isopoda/ligia)

**Class Cirripedia** [barnacles](othercrust/ciriipedia/cirripedia)    

**Class Ostracoda** [mussel shrimps](othercrust/ostracoda/ostracoda)

from Edward E.Ruppert, Richard S. Fox, Robert D. Barnes. 2004.*[InvertebrateZoology](http://www.amazon.com/gp/redirectl?ie=UTF8&location=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.amazon.com%2FInvertebrate-Zoology-Functional-Evolutionary-Approach%2Fdp%2F0030259827%3Fie%3DUTF8%26s%3Dbooks%26qid%3D1175579272%26sr%3D8-2&tag=wildsingapo08-20&linkCode=ur2&camp=1789&creative=9325)![](http://www.assoc-amazon.com/e/ir?t=wildsingapo08-20&l=ur2&o=1)* Brooks/Cole of Thomson Learning Inc., 7th Edition. pp. 963


{% include img-grid.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}
**Crusty cast-off:** Like other Arthropods, crustaceans have
to shed their hard outer skeleton (exoskeleton) in order to grow bigger.
The process is called moulting and is quite fascinating. [More
about moulting](moulting). **Human uses:** Crustaceans are eaten by people everywhere.
Barnacles grow on any surface in the sea, including ships, water pipes
and other important infrastructure. Thus these tiny animals can affect
human activities in a big way.  
  
## Status and threats
Sadly, many of our beautiful and fascinating crustaceans are listed among the
endangered animals of Singapore. Like other marine creatures, they
are vulnerable to habitat loss due to reclamation or human activities
along the coast that pollute the water. They are also vulnerable to
trampling by careless visitors and over-collection for food and for
their shells can affect local populations.

