INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/echinodermata/echinoidea/urchin/bicolor.md', 'bicolor', 'Two-toned salmacis urchin', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This spicy-looking red sea urchin was seen once along an East Coast Park shore that has since been affected by coastal works.

## Features

The one we saw had a body diameter 5-8cm, yellowish. Spines
(1.5-2cm)
longer than the more common White sea urchin, and banded white and red. Spines on the upper side are sharp. Spines on the underside have spade-like tips. It has long tube feet.

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}

---

**Two-toned salmacis urchins on Singapore shores**

On
[wildsingapore flickr](http://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/)

- [Photos](https://www.flickr.com/search/?user_id=54527470@N00&sort=date-posted-desc&view_all=1&text=Salmacis bicolor)
for free download
- [Map of sightings in Singapore](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/tags/Salmacis%2520bicolor/map?&fLat=1.2619&fLon=103.8661&zl=11&order_by=recent)

---

**Acknowlegment**

Grateful thanks to Dr Frederic Ducarme for identifying these sea urchins.


#EndEditable
