INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/mollusca/gastropoda/limpets/guamensis.md', 'guamensis', 'Guam false limpet', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This little limpet is commonly seen in small groups on boulders and hard surfaces on many of our shores, usually near the high water mark.

## Features

1-2cm. Shell thin conical with a pointed tip and many fine white ribs. There is no hole at the top of the shell. Body plain white.
It is a false limpet and breathes through lungs instead of gills.
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}
**Limpet Babies:**
*Siphonaria*
limpets lay eggs in circular or coiling jelly-like masses that contain thousands of eggs suspended in a gelatinous matrix, attached to a hard surface. The free-swimming limpet larvae have a little spiral shell like other ''normal'' snails. As they develop, the shell flattens and becomes umbrella-shaped.

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}
---

**Guam false limpets on Singapore shores**

On
[wildsingapore flickr](http://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/)

- [Photos](https://www.flickr.com/search/?user_id=54527470@N00&sort=date-posted-desc&safe_search=3&view_all=1&text=Siphonaria guamensis)
for free download
- [Map of sightings in Singapore](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/tags/Siphonaria%2520guamensis/map?&fLat=1.2622&fLon=103.8674&zl=11&order_by=recent)

---

**Other sightings on Singapore shores**

---
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery3 %}
---

### References


- Tan Siong Kiat and Henrietta P. M. Woo, 2010
*Preliminary Checklist of The Molluscs of Singapore*
(pdf), Raffles Museum of Biodiversity Research, National University of Singapore.
- Tan, K. S. & L. M. Chou, 2000.
*A Guide to the Common Seashells of Singapore*
. Singapore Science Centre. 160 pp.
