INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/mollusca/gastropoda/mitridae/undulosa.md', 'undulosa', 'Waved mitre snails', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This bullet-shaped burrowing snail was seen on Changi and Chek Jawa. Initially in some numbers, then dwindling away.

## Features

2-5cm. Shell thick with fine ridges in a cross-hatched pattern. The shell has a dark ''skin'' which can peel off. There is notch at the tip of the shell for the siphon to emerge. The animal is white with a broad foot and short tentacles. Operculum small and brown.

**What does it eat?**
Mitre snails are carnivorous and many feed on worms. They may also scavenge.
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}
**Baby mitres:**
The snail
was seen in groups of several individuals with small egg capsules laid on hard surfaces nearby
.

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}
---

**Waved mitre snails on Singapore shores**

On
[wildsingapore flickr](http://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/)

- [Photos](https://www.flickr.com/search/?user_id=54527470@N00&view_all=1&text=Pterygia undulosa)
for free download
- [Map of sightings in Singapore](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/tags/Pterygia%2520undulosa/map?&fLat=1.264&fLon=103.8653&zl=11&order_by=recent)

---

**Other sightings on Singapore shores**

---
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery3 %}
---

**Family Mitridae recorded for Singapore**
from Tan Siong Kiat and Henrietta P. M. Woo, 2010
*Preliminary Checklist of The Molluscs of Singapore*
.

^from
[WORMS](http://www.marinespecies.org/)
.

**Family Mitridae**

*Cancilla filiaris=^Domiporta filaris

Cancilla granatina=^Domiporta granatina

Mitra ambigua

Mitra fraga

Mitra mitra

Mitra proscissa

Mitra puncticulata

Mitra rosacea

Mitra scutulata

Mitra stictica

Neocancilla circula

Pterygia dactylus

Pterygia undulosa*
(Waved mitre snail)
*Subcancilla interlirata

Ziba insculpta*

---

**Acknowledgement**
Thanks to Neo Mei Lin for sorting out the identification of this snail on
[her blog](http://psychedelic-nature.blogspot.sg/2012/08/a-week-of-taxonomy-fieldtrip-to-changi.html)
, and to Tan Siong Kiat for identifying it.
