INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/mollusca/slugs/notaspidae/pleurobranchus.md', 'pleurobranchus', 'Forskal''s sidegill slug', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This humungous slug is sometimes seen, near reefs and seagrass areas. Sometimes seen buried just beneath the sand. On Cyrene, large numbers have been seen at some times of the year. According to Dr Bill Rudman, it is often found in quite large populations in shallow lagoons, reef crests and pools and sea grass beds.

## Features

20-30cm. The large slug has a body texture of flat polygonal bumps with faint white lines forming circles. Colour variable from black, dark maroon to lighter shades of brown and orange. It has a pair of tubular rhinophores on its head. The large single gill is found on the right side between the mantle and the foot. The underside is smooth and unmarked. The slug appears to secrete a slime that feels acidic and is hard to rub off your hand.
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}
**What does it eat?**
Although it is not known what this species eats, other species of
*Pleurobranchus*
are reported to feed on ascidians. Indeed, on our shores large numbers are sometimes seen among dense beds of
[Green gum drop ascidians](../../../../ascidiacea/greengumdrop)
.

**What eats it?**
According to Dr Rudman, chitinous plates identified as the jaw plates of this sea slug had been found in the stomach of a turtle.

**Baby slugs:**
They have been seen laying egg masses in white or pink frilly ribbons.

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}[---](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/23358180213/){% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery3 %}

