INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/plants/seaweed/chlorophyta/chaetomorpha.md', 'chaetomorpha', '''Bee hoon'' green seaweed', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This green seaweed does look like our local noodle favourite, ''bee hoon'' (vermicelli)! It is seen on many of our shores, growing entangled among seagrasses or other seaweeds. It ''blooms'' seasonally, sometimes drifting up on the shores in large tangled bundles. It may also form a bright green mossy carpet under mangrove trees!

## Features

Filaments fine, long (40cm or more) and unbranched that grow entangled among other seaweeds. Each filament is made up of a single row of cylindrical cells joined together end to end. The cells are longer than they are wide: thus the filament resembles bamboo when seen under a microscope. Bright light green to dark green, sometimes olive green.

According to
[AlgaeBase](http://www.algaebase.org/search/genus/detail/?genus_id=35)
, there are more than 50 current
*Chaetomorpha*
species.

**Sometimes confused with**
[Hairy green seaweed](bryopsis)
(*Bryopsis*
sp.) which has much shorter filaments and appears feathery. Here''s more on
[how to tell apart some green seaweeds](cffeathery)
.

## Human uses

It is used as animal feed and eaten raw by people.

![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/plants/seaweed/chlorophyta/chaetomorpha/020225cjd7289m3.jpg)

Large piles of the seaweed
*Chek Jawa, Feb 02*
![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/plants/seaweed/chlorophyta/chaetomorpha/020224cjcpd7156m3.jpg)

*Chek Jawa, Feb 02*

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}
---
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}

**Species are difficult to positively identify without close examination of internal parts.

On this website, they are grouped by external features for convenience of display.*

---

**Bee hoon green seaweeds on Singapore shores**

