INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/plants/seaweed/rhodophyta/catstail.md', 'catstail', 'Cat''s tail red seaweed ', 'published', '
## Where seen?
These beautiful feathery seaweeds are sometimes seen in on our Southern shores, attached to coral rubble near reefs. In another part of its lifecycle, could it look like the
[Cotton candy red seaweed](fluffy)
?

## Features

Clusters of feathery ''stems'' (6-8cm long). Each ''stem'' has side branches covered in fine filaments that result in a feathery appearance resembling the tail of a cat or a feather duster. Colours pinkish, maroon sometimes purplish.

According to
[AlgaeBase](http://www.algaebase.org/search/genus/detail/?genus_id=5)
, there are currently 3
*Aspargopsis*
species.

Human uses: It is used as animal feed, fertiliser and human food. It is also reported to have antibacterial and antibiotic properties, and to be used to treat livestock diseases.

*![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/plants/seaweed/rhodophyta/catstail/050329sntg3861m3a.jpg)

Sentosa, Mar 05*

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}

**Seaweed species are difficult to positively identify without microscopic examination.

On this website, they are grouped by external features for convenience of display.*

---

