INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/porifera/tube.md', 'tube', 'Tubular sponge', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This tubular sponge is commonly seen on our undisturbed Northern shores, growing on coral rubble. Also sometimes seen on undisturbed Southern shores.
## Features

The entire sponge 10-15cm wide with closely packed narrow hollow tubes (4-6cm tall about 1-2cm wide). In some, the tubes may have short spikes on the sides. A wide range of colours from beige, lemon yellow, pale to bright blue, pale green and purple.

**Sometimes confused with**
[Spiky tubular sponges](tubespiky)
(*Gelliodes*
sp.) which have neat spikes regularly arranged around their hollow tubes forming a star-like shape around the large hole at the tibe of the tube.
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}

---
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}**Species are difficult to positively identify without close examination.

On this website, they are grouped by external features for convenience of display.*

---

**Tubular sponges on Singapore shores**

On
[wildsingapore flickr](http://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/)

- [Photos](https://www.flickr.com/search/?q=Haliclona+sp.+Tubular&w=54527470@N00&s=rec)
for free download
- [Map of sightings in Singapore](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/tags/Haliclona%2520sp.%2520Tubular/map?&fLat=1.2622&fLon=103.8661&zl=11&order_by=recent)

---

**Other sightings on Singapore shores**

---
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery3 %}
---
