INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('entries', '_entries/vertebrates/fish/ophichthidae/wormeel.md', 'wormeel', 'Worm-eel', 'published', '
## Where seen?
This small worm-like fish is often seen near coral rubble where there are lots of hiding places, especially on our Southern shores. A secretive fish, it is usually active only at night. Sightings are usually brief as it wriggles rapidly past and disappears into some crevice or hole.

## Features

To about 20cm long, those seen were about 10-12cm. Body long narrow tubular. It is superbly adapted for burrowing in sand and hunting in small holes and crevices. It has no scales, no pectoral fins. Eyes and mouth are small, the snout is pointed and nostrils downward pointing. The tail tip is bony and sharp so it can burrow quickly, both forwards and backwards! It swims by moving the body in S-shapes, rather like a sea snake.

**Sometimes mistaken for**
[worms](../../../../worm/worm)
or
[sea snakes](../../../snakes/hydrophiidae/laticauda)
. Here''s more on
[how to tell apart sea snakes, eels and eel-like animals](../../../../glossary/eelsnake)
.
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}
{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.gallery3 %}
Worm eel caught a small fish!

**What do they eat?**
Most species are burrowing, spending most of their time in the sand and among crevices in coral rubble. They hunt small fishes and crustaceans by smell.

---

**Worm eels on Singapore shores**

On
[wildsingapore flickr](http://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/)

- [Photos](https://www.flickr.com/search/?text=Muraenichthys&sort=date-taken-desc&user_id=54527470@N00)
for free download
- [Map of sightings in Singapore](https://www.flickr.com/photos/wildsingapore/tags/Muraenichthys/map?&fLat=1.2622&fLon=103.8661&zl=11&order_by=recent)

