INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/OurWildSpaces2.0/baharforest.md', 'baharforest', 'Bahar Forest', 'published', '
## Ecological Significance

The forests flanking Jalan Bahar sit along one of the only ecological corridors between Western and Central Catchment habitats, which continue to be compromised by the loss of sites like Tengah Forest. Although only about 0.6 ha of the land intended for upcoming development are of native-dominated composition, the characteristic streams and waterlogged swampy conditions of this landscape have given rise to unique biodiversity throughout.

![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-2.jpg)

In terms of dragonfly diversity at Sites A and B, recorded rarities like the Pixie (*Brachygonia oculata*) and the photosensitive darners (*Aeshnidae*) are not widely found outside Nature Reserves due to specialised microhabitat requirements. Notably, a darner species not recorded from anywhere else in Singapore was encountered within the Nanyang Technological University campus, likely drawn to the light spillover from campus buildings but originating from Bahar or its neighbouring forests. Such findings of rarities indicate there is yet more diversity to uncover in dynamic, recovering habitats, even after being subject to comprehensive EIA studies.

{% include justifiedgallery.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}

Flashing displays of the nationally endangered asynchronous firefly (*Pteroptyx valida*) were encountered across Site B, indicating suitable swamp-like habitat for the larvae of this species which usually opts for river bends and mangroves instead. Findings of thriving Harlequin butterflies, a similarly endangered species, can likely also be explained by a combination of swampy conditions and their abundant host plants in both sites.

{% include justifiedgallery.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}

Mammals living in the Western Catchment forests are already affected by habitat fragmentation, particularly through road collisions and barriers created by urban development. Both the elusive Leopard Cat and Sunda Pangolin are found in the Bahar forests, with pangolin roadkill recorded regularly in nearby areas, including around the NTU campus. Without protected links between Singapore’s western and central forests, these populations risk becoming increasingly isolated from other populations on the mainland.

## Why is This Area Threatened?

![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-5.jpg)

620 ha of land intended for the upcoming Jurong Innovation District includes the CleanTech Park (Site A) and Bahar precincts (Site B) which are hence slated for clearing. Between the two sites, 52 hectares will reportedly be developed for “advanced manufacturing activities including robotics, medical technology, and smart logistics” (MTI, 3 Feb 2026). More broadly, landuse planning in the Jurong region supports its industrial intensification, given the proximity of NTU and the construction of new MRT stations intended to service this envisioned manufacturing hub.

## Our Hopes for the Site

![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-8.jpg)

The public feedback window for the EIA on Bahar’s forests closed in December 2025. The decision to retain a proportion of land should not just prioritise native tree cover alone but reflect the importance of streams and scrubland across Site B’s extent which support specialised and rare fauna. The challenge of mitigating impacts to this biodiversity stronghold presents an opportunity to involve relevant research and nature interest groups in assessing and conserving species groups usually neglected by traditional EIAs, such as beetles and other invertebrates. Generous **buffers** around firefly trees should be instated not just to mitigate the effects of urban light pollution but also to protect larval habitat. Tree-planting exercises initiated to mitigate **edge effects** and the flushing of wildlife onto roads are also avenues for collaboration with student and nature groups in the Bahar vicinity.', '{
  "title": "Bahar Forest",
  "summary": null,
  "layout": "OWS2",
  "coverimg": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-1.jpg",
  "galleries": {
    "gallery1": [
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-3.jpg"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-4.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "gallery2": [
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-6.jpg"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-7.jpg"
      }
    ]
  }
}', 'https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bahar-Forest-1.jpg', '2026-09-27T06:31:04.143Z');
INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/OurWildSpaces2.0/bukitbrowncemetery.md', 'bukitbrowncemetery', 'Bukit Brown Cemetery', 'published', '
## Ecological Significance

The Bukit Brown Forest is primarily an abandoned-land secondary forest, with clear evidence of past human disturbance, indicated by the presence of banana, dumb cane, African Tulip, and other kampung-associated species. While some areas are densely vegetated, much of the canopy remains dominated by cultivated species.

{% include justifiedgallery.html items=page.galleries.gallery1 %}

There are also multiple freshwater streams that run through Bukit Brown and support small fish populations. In areas where the canopy is closed, there are observable signs of **understorey recruitment**, highlighting promising forest recovery. Bukit Brown has the potential to function as an ecological **stepping stone** which enhances connectivity to the Botanic Gardens and even further south toward Dempsey, though it only supports wildlife capable of flight.

## Heritage Significance

Named after George Henry Brown, the site was originally intended to be developed as a coffee and nutmeg plantation, but the venture failed due to Singapore’s tropical climate. The land was later acquired by the Hokkien Ong clan, who planned to establish a self-sufficient village for poorer clan members. Instead, the area was designated solely as a burial ground and became known as Seh Ong Cemetery. A portion of the cemetery was then acquired to form Bukit Brown Municipal Cemetery. In its early years, Chinese families were hesitant to conduct burials due to small plot sizes and perceived disregard for feng shui principles. However, following improvements to plot layouts and several high-profile burials, the number of interments increased.

{% include justifiedgallery.html items=page.galleries.gallery2 %}

The cemetery is the resting place of many prominent Chinese pioneers, including Eu Kong, mother of Eu Tong Sen, as well as Lim Nee Soon and Chew Boon Lay - names that remain familiar today through the places named after them. Bukit Brown also contains the graves of numerous individuals connected to World War II, reflecting the cemetery’s proximity to sites associated with the final battles before the British surrender in 1942. Tombs themselves offer a window into Singapore’s multicultural past. Many are ornately decorated with stone lions, Sikh guards, cherubic angels, liberty bells and other motifs drawn from different cultural and religious traditions. The Adam Rd and Bukit Brown sites look markedly denuded of vegetation from aerial photos from the 1950s (above). It was after the cemetery’s official closure in 1973 that secondary forest growth reclaimed the site after the cessation of active landscaping and maintenance. **The dense growth seen in parts of both sites are at most 70 years old**, showing the incredible regeneration potential of these sites, and elsewhere.

## Why is This Area Threatened?

![](https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-6.jpg)

Parts of Bukit Brown are zoned for housing. The construction of a shell
station for the Thomson-East Coast Line station along Jalan Mashhor
indicate that future developments are likely to occur here.

## Our Hopes for the Site

More vegetation could be retained at the fringe of the golf course north of Bukit Brown. The west of Bukit Brown should also be retained to preserve connectivity in accordance with NParks'' **Ecological Profiling Exercise** (pending findings from any future Environmental Impact Assessments in the area). There could be assisted rehabilitation of the forest, with removal of introduced species and reforestation of native species. Finally, the streams in the site could be naturalised, and those with species of **Conservation Significance** retained.

{% include justifiedgallery.html items=page.galleries.gallery3 %}', '{
  "title": "Bukit Brown Cemetery",
  "summary": null,
  "layout": "OWS2",
  "coverimg": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-1.jpg",
  "galleries": {
    "gallery1": [
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-2.jpg"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-3.jpg"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-4.jpg"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-5.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "gallery2": [
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-7.jpg"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/OWS2/Bukit-Brown-8.jpg"
