---

Some corals
with big ring-shaped corallites covered in thick fleshy tissue
may look similar. With the flesh totally retracted, anchor corals may also be mistaken for these corals. Here''s more on how to tell them apart.
 

---

![](mussidae/lobophyllia/130824smkd2605m3.jpg)
![](merulinidae/caulastraea/040606kusg0480m3.jpg)
![](euphylliidae/paraancora/110422tbmd9794m3.jpg)
**[Lobed
brain coral](mussidae/lobophyllia.htm)***Lobophyllia* sp.  
Family Mussidae
**[Trumpet
coral](merulinidae/caulastraea.htm)***Caulastraea* sp.  
Family Merulinidae
[**Branching
anchor coral**](euphylliidae/paraancora.htm)  
*Euphyllia paraancora*  
Family Euphyllidae
![](mussidae/lobophyllia/130824smkd2605m3a.jpg)
![](merulinidae/caulastraea/040606kusg0480m3b.jpg)
![](euphylliidae/paraancora/110422tbmd9794m3a.jpg)
Corallite
large with large spiky teeth.
Corallite
small with fine teeth.
Corallites
trumpet-shaped   
(phaceloid) and branching.
![](mussidae/lobophyllia/130824smkd2605m3b.jpg)
![](merulinidae/caulastraea/040606kusg0480m3c.jpg)
![](euphylliidae/paraancora/110422tbmd9794m3c.jpg)
Tissue
thick and very fleshy,   
with bands of fine ''pimples'' (not smooth),  
usually uniformly coloured.
Tissue
fleshy but not very thick,   
smooth (no pimples),   
often with bands in contrasting colours.
No fleshy tissue. Instead, has  
polyps with long tentacles (2-3cm) and a U-shaped tip.

