INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/habitats/adinandra-belukar.md', 'adinandra-belukar', 'Adinandra Belukar', 'published', '
## Why the Name?

Adinandra belukar means "Bush of Adinandra". [*Adinandra dumosa*](/entries/plants/coastal/adinandra/dumosa/), also known as the ''Tiup-tiup'', is the main plant species in this habitat''s characterisation.

## Plants Commonly Found Here

### **Main characterising plants**
- [Tiup-tiup (*Adinandra dumosa*)](/entries/plants/coastal/adinandra/dumosa/)
- [Resam Fern (*Dicranopteris linearis*)](/entries/plants/tracheophyta/polypodiopsida/gleicheniales/gleicheniaceae/dicranopteris/linearis/)

These plants, if covering the land in abundance, would tell you that you are standing in an Adinandra Belukar habitat.

### Other **associated** Plants
- *Smilax setosa*
- *Fragraea fragrans*
- *Campnosperma auriculatum*
- *Macaranga gigantea*
- *Macaranga heynei*

These plants are associated with Adinandra belukar habitats, but are not a tell-tale sign. These plants are all native, with all except the first being trees. The presence of these trees show that the habitat you''re in is quite early in the process of succession.

## Animals Commonly Found Here
- Pin-striped Tit Babbler
- Rufous Woodpecker
- Greater Racket-tailed Drongo

These birds are associated with young secondary forests, but still require ecological connectivity to other habitats, and also require a sizable forest to survive. These birds may not do well in exotic-dominated secondary forests, or very small forest patches.

## Forest Succession

Adinandra belukar habitats enter a form of "stasis" where they struggle to succeed to the next step, which is the mature lowland forest. Young secondary forests are supposed to, over centuries, transform or succeed into more mature forests of even greater ecological complexity. But the [Tiup-tiup](/entries/plants/coastal/adinandra/dumosa/) and [Resam Fern](/entries/plants/tracheophyta/polypodiopsida/gleicheniales/gleicheniaceae/dicranopteris/linearis/) hinder this succession process.

The [Resam Fern](/entries/plants/tracheophyta/polypodiopsida/gleicheniales/gleicheniaceae/dicranopteris/linearis/) is said to have allelopathy, where it releases toxins through its roots to ensure that the substrate can only be tolerated by its own kind.', '{
  "title": "Adinandra Belukar",
  "description": "The Adinandra Belukar is dominated by the Tiup-tiup or Resam Fern, and struggles to transform into a mature lowland forest.",
  "keywords": "native, young secondary forest, succession, ecology, adinandra dumosa, dicranopteris linearis, adinandra belukar",
  "layout": "default",
  "date": "2026-09-18"
}', '', '2026-09-18');
INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/habitats/coastalvegetation.md', 'coastalvegetation', 'Coastal Forests', 'published', '
## Unique Circumstances

![The canopy of a primary coastal forest in Datai Bay, Langkawi.](https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/CoastalForest/CoastalForest.jpg)

Coastal vegetation is its own kind of forest. Singapore used to have primary coastal forests sprinkled along its coastlines where cliffs would be. However, most of these coastal forests have been destroyed due to human activity.

Coastal forests form when they:
- experience high amounts of constant wind
- are near the coastline

Coastal vegetation have adapted to live in windy conditions, which are very different from our [lowland](/articles/habitats/lowlandforest/) forests, which have adapted to live in very shaded and humid environments. They adapt by either growing very fast to rapidly replace limbs lost to windshear, or by growing to be flexible and tough to resist fracturing.

## Plants Commonly Found Here
- [Jambu Air Laut (*Syzygium grande*)](/entries/plants/coastal/syzygium/grande/)
- [Ketapang (*Terminala catappa*)](/entries/plants/coastal/terminalia/catappa/)
- [Casuarina (*Casuarina equisetifolia*)](/entries/plants/coastal/casuarina/equisetifolia/)

These plants, if covering the land in abundance, would tell you that you are standing in a coastal forest.

### Other **Associated** Plants

- Mengkuang Bunga (*Benstonea affinis*)

### Rare Coastal Plants

- [Lukeh / Seashore Bat Lily (*Tacca leontopetaloides*)](/entries/plants/coastal/tacca/leontopetaloides/)

### Animals Commonly Found Here

- Plantain Squirrels
- Greater Racket-tailed Drongo
- Banded Woodpecker

Forest-dependent or forest-adapted species found in native-dominated secondary forests would also be found here. These animals are usually travelling from or to other lowland habitats like the mangroves or lowland forests.

### Coastal **Associated** Animals

- Pied Imperial Pigeon
- Pink-necked Green Pigeon
- Asian Red-eyed Bulbul

Coastal forest animals are strong fliers, as flying is the main mode of transport between islands or coastal forests. Examples where coastal island-hopping happens often is in Polynesia, the Philippines, or Indonesia, where small islands support a singular main population.', '{
  "title": "Coastal Forests",
  "description": "A forest type that grows in very dry areas, near the coast or on cliffs.",
  "keywords": "native, coastal forest, primary forest, ecology",
  "layout": "default",
  "date": "2026-09-18",
  "coverimg": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/CoastalForest/CoastalForestCanopy.jpg"
}', 'https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/CoastalForest/CoastalForestCanopy.jpg', '2026-09-18');
