INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/habitats/freshwaterbody.md', 'freshwaterbody', 'Freshwater Body', 'published', '
Freshwater bodies form when the water table is higher than the substrate. In Singapore, our largest freshwater bodies like our reservoirs, are artificial. The first reservoir created in Singapore is the Macritchie Reservoir, which was created in 1867.

## Biodiversity and Reservoirs

Reservoirs are large surfaces of water, with nothing above the water. After the creation of reservoirs within our Central Catchment Nature Reserve (CCNR), habitat fragmentation happened and an extinction debt was created. Creating the reservoirs meant logging and removing all trees below the projected waterline, and this removed the ability for less-mobile species to travel between the CCNR. The White-chested Babbler populations on our mainland have died off due to extensive inbreeding, caused by [habitat fragmentation](/articles/concepts/habitatfragmentation/).

Singapore is one of little countries with potable tap water, but this is not without sacrifices. The Public Utilities Board (PUB) likes to keep the water within these reservoirs squeaky clean to reduce cost on filtering and processing of the raw water, as it gets transformed into our potable tap water. This means that reservoirs have relatively little biomass within the water body. No weeds, wetland plants, floating plants, or any trees. Because of such extensive measures by PUB, reservoirs have very little biodiversity in general.', '{
  "title": "Freshwater Body",
  "description": "A freshwater body, typically found in the lowest portion of catchments.",
  "keywords": "pond, lake, freshwater, fish, dragonflies",
  "layout": "default",
  "date": "2026-09-18",
  "coverimg": null
}', '', '2026-09-18');
INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/habitats/fwsf.md', 'fwsf', 'Freshwater Swamp Forest', 'published', '
## Unique Circumstances

Freshwater swamp forests are very unique, where they always have waterlogged soil requiring the flora within them to adapt. Most plants take in oxygen through the roots, and waterlogging hinders this process. Thus, adaptations have been conjured so these plants can respire in other areas through the atmosphere.

Similar to mangroves, the trees within these habitats adapted to have special organs to allow them to respire.

- prop/stilt roots
- lenticels
- pneumatophores

## Plants Commonly Found Here

### **Main characterising plants**

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.freshwaterobligates %}

These species are known as freshwater swamp forest **obligates**, which means they live only in the freshwater swamp forest. They would dry up in normal lowland forest areas, and lowland forest species would drown in a freshwater swamp forest.

### Other **associated** Plants

- Syzygium cerasiforme
- Campnosperma auriculatum

These species are known as freshwater swamp forest **associates**, which means they can live in a freshwater swamp forest, but also in other areas. *Sygyzium cerasiforme*, for example, occurs along the Pan-Island Expressway (PIE) as tall hedges, but can also live within freshwater swamp forests without drowning.

## Animals Commonly Found Here

- Birds
    - Chestnut-winged Babbler
    - White-chested Babbler
    - Short-tailed Babbler
- Fishes
    - Whitespot Panchax
    - Dwarf Rasbora
    - Harlequin Rasbora
    - Einthoven''s Rasbora
    - Twospot Rasbora
    - Spanner Barb

These birds are always within vicinity of a freshwater swamp forest, but the Chestnut-winged Babbler lives exclusively in the undergrowth of a freshwater swamp forest. They do not explore much into the surrounding lowland forests. Keen to see them? Go explore any freshwater swamp forest habitats around Sime Track in the early morning!

Fishes of the freshwater swamp forest also have to adapt to the absurdly low pH of the water. Due to a high tannin content and low water hardiness (due to lack of exposed rocks), the water pH ranges between 4.6 to 5.5.

## Forest Succession

Freshwater swamp forests are very selective in nature, and only allow freshwater swamp forest obligates or associates to survive. Thus, similar to mangroves, freshwater swamp forests do not have any succession stages.', '{
  "title": "Freshwater Swamp Forest",
  "description": "A seasonally flooded, or permanently flooded forest type with specially adapted plants.",
  "keywords": "native, swamp forest, succession, ecology, primary forest, flooded",
  "layout": "default",
  "date": "2026-09-18",
  "coverimg": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/FWSF/FWSF2.JPG",
  "galleries": {
    "freshwaterobligates": [
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": "The Glochidion zeylanicum can be identified by its red petioles, and the fact that it grows almost exclusively in soft waterlogged soil."
      },
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": "Alstonia spatulata and Alstonia pneumatophora are freshwater swamp obligates. They are distinct for their leaf whorl arrangements."
      },
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": null
      },
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": null
      }
    ]
  }
}', 'https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/FWSF/FWSF2.JPG', '2026-09-18');
INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/habitats/garden.md', 'garden', 'Gardens and Parks', 'published', '
Gardens and Parks have varying levels of ecological complexity. The ends of the spectrum have the Singapore Botanic Gardens on the end of high complexity, and Jurong Central Park on the end of lower complexity. However, the least ecologically complex park still has more biodiversity than any cycling road on our Park Connector Network (PCN), as well as our Nature Ways. This is due to size. Parks are always wider or bigger, allowing for more space and at times, a conducive climate within the park.

## Community Gardens

Community gardens are common, and almost every HDB estate has it. These gardens are made up of lots of ornamental flowering plants, and plants with edible fruits.

## Neighbourhood Parks

Neighbourhood parks are very urban environments, with the latest addition being the expansion of the Istana Park. These parks can hold a substantial amount of migratory bird species, as the ecological structure and functional niches accommodated for are similar to the woodlands back in East Asia.

## Animals Commonly Found Here

{% include img-wrap-group.html items=page.galleries.commonbirds %}

These birds are truly everywhere. They love to feed on anything found in these areas, especially within community gardens.

