INSERT INTO content_items (collection, path, slug, title, status, body, metadata_json, cover_image, published_at) VALUES ('articles', '_articles/habitats/mangrove.md', 'mangrove', 'Mangrove', 'published', '
![A typical mangrove river. (Datai Bay, Langkawi)](https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/Mangroves/MangroveRiver.jpg)

## Plants Commonly Found Here

### **Main characterising plants**

- Avicennia spp. (Usually front mangrove)
- Rhizophora spp.
- Brugeria spp. (Usually back mangrove)
- Nipah Palm (*Nypa fruticans*)

There are a lot more mangrove species. Globally, there are 70 species, 35 of which are in Singapore. There are also non-mangrove trees which are associated with the mangrove habitat. All these plants have adapted to growing in the tidal zone, with high salinity and anoxic muddy substrate.

Traits of these adaptations include:
- secretion of salt on the leaves
- a wide root base for stability
- breathing points above the waterline
    - descenders from high branches
    - prop/stilt roots
    - lenticels
    - pneumatophores

## Animals Commonly Found Here

### **Main characterising animals**

- Birds
    - Black-and-red Broadbill
    - Copper-throated Sunbird
    - Ruby-cheeked Sunbird
    - Mangrove Pitta
- Insects
    - Mangrove Jewel Bug (*Calliphara nobilis*)
- Crabs
    - *Parasesarma eumolpe*
    - *Parasesarma peninsulare*
- Fishes
    - *Eugnathogobius polyepis*

Mangroves have mangrove specialists. These animals live exclusively in the mangroves, and do not reside in any other habitats.

### Other Animals Found Here

- Brown-throated Sunbird
- House Crow
- Asian Glossy Starling

These animals occur in mangroves, but do not live only in mangroves. They can be found in other habitats as well.

## Forest Succession

Mangrove, unlike terrestrial forests, do NOT need to undergo forest succession. There are no primary or secondary mangrove forests, and mangrove trees are sorted by zonation to allow for different species to live in different functional niches instead.

## Mangrove Zonation

Mangroves are never flat. There is always some form of slope or topography, and this is what zonation relies on. Different species of mangrove trees have ', '{
  "title": "Mangrove",
  "description": "A forest type that grows in very dry areas, near the coast or on cliffs.",
  "keywords": "native, coastal forest, primary forest, ecology",
  "layout": "default",
  "date": "2026-09-18",
  "coverimg": "https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/Mangroves/Mangroves.jpg",
  "galleries": {
    "mangrovepropagules": [
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": "Avicennia propagules scatter themselves all over the shore, and gamble on having some reaching the correct zone in the front of the mangrove."
      },
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": "Rhizophora propagules are huge, which means they don''t get to float to the back of the mangrove on the highest tides."
      },
      {
        "url": null,
        "caption": "Brugeria propagules are so tiny, they float all the way to the back of the mangrove on high tides."
      }
    ]
  }
}', 'https://images.naturehungry.sg/photos/Habitats/Mangroves/Mangroves.jpg', '2026-09-18');
